shadowfetch-hwscan (2.1.2-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * New component for 2.1.2 "Fire Edition": the hardware + AI
    compatibility scanner.
  * /usr/libexec/shadowfetch-hwscan: unprivileged reads only (sysfs,
    /proc, pci.ids) plus exactly two time-boxed subprocesses
    (vulkaninfo --json, nvidia-smi; 3 s each, failure -> "unknown").
    No polkit anywhere; works from the live session and from Ignition.
  * Honest VRAM split: AMD measured from sysfs, Intel iGPUs "share
    system RAM" and never get a number, NVIDIA post-driver measured via
    the nvidia-smi CSV pattern (no NVML bindings), Vulkan heap sums
    labeled "estimated", everything else "unknown" with the copy
    "VRAM: measured after driver install". Software rasterizers
    (llvmpipe/swrast) can never masquerade as a GPU estimate.
  * Deterministic recommendation table with the judge folds applied:
    physical cores below 6 downgrade every class above Whisper by one
    tier on CPU paths ("slower processor - expect well below typical
    speeds"); the founder's "comfortably run 7B" sentence is reserved
    for tiers with 8 GB+ VRAM full offload, and 16 GB CPU-only machines
    get the honest "relaxed pace" line instead; "reduced context or
    quantization" always carries its plain-words gloss.
  * shadowfetch-hwscan.service (oneshot, hardened, IPAddressDeny=any)
    writes world-readable /var/lib/shadowfetch/hwscan.json each boot;
    the firstboot GPU flow restarts it after a driver install so
    estimated/unknown flips to measured.
  * 47 fixture-pinned unit tests (rule table rows including the
    low-core and 16 GB-CPU wording rows, both vulkaninfo layouts,
    nvidia-smi CSV, sysfs trees, exact JSON schema) run at build time.

 -- Shadowfetch Project <signing@shadowfetch.com>  Thu, 30 Jul 2026 12:00:00 -0400
